From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 11:43:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ks_code_stats: count the memory compiled code retains, by
 form
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The GC's byte count sees only GcBoxes, not the source text, bytecode,
inline caches and source maps a code block owns, so nothing could say
how much of a page's memory is compiled code, or how much of it never
ran. That is the number that decides whether moving cold code out of
wasm memory is worth building.

Per-thread counters, charged when a code block or source text is
created and refunded when it drops: blocks and blocks that ran,
retained source texts and their bytes, packed and expanded bytecode
(first run moves a block from one to the other), built inline caches,
source maps, and the rest of each block's own arrays. Shared strings,
scopes and nested blocks are not charged to the block holding them.

beep-boop-🤖
